{"step_index":61,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-29T02:41:27Z","input_tokens":2998,"cache_read_tokens":54247,"output_tokens":3601,"thinking":"Initial project concept centered on a Sci-Fi Supercar or F1 2035 theme, starting with a Blender file (`scifi_f1_2035.blend`) based on sci-fi references. Early development involved GT supercar prototypes, such as `supercar_gt_v2` and `supercar_gt_v3`.\n\n\nAddressing previous version deficiencies, a complete rebuild emphasizes realistic F1 car features. Implementing parametric wheels with accurate dimensions and materials (tires, rims, central locknuts) improves visual fidelity. Furthermore, a new braking system incorporating carbon-ceramic discs and air ducts enhances the simulation's realism.\nThe aerodynamics received a significant overhaul, incorporating a functional DRS, narrowed endplates, and a Venturi undertray. Conformal decals replace floating cards, enhancing the model's visual integration. A procedural carbon fiber material and heat-tinted titanium exhaust add further realism.\nA sophisticated, hierarchical rigging system ensures accurate animation. The aim is a concise hierarchy that facilitates realistic motion, unlike the previous iteration's problematic drift angles.\n\n\nRigging now accounts for 236 parts, rigidly bound to corresponding bones; expression drivers enable real-time viewport control of key vehicle dynamics. The track is a 1779-meter procedural circuit with realistic geometry, now fixed from the self-intersecting loop.\n\nVehicle dynamics have been refined for authenticity, limiting the maximum slip angle and incorporating realistic pitch, roll, and suspension behavior. Soundtrack integration with *After Hours* will be achieved via music beat-cut techniques and multi-camera cinematography.\n\n\nA sequence of 10 camera shots, precisely synchronized across 720 frames, is the current focus, showcasing the F1 car's dynamics. The scene includes a livery up